                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "18258bee-4956-3ddd-aaf5-cbc61bb227e8",
                        "entry_id": "84cb31ce-963f-3ea7-998b-9157212a5e62",
                        "glosses": [
                            "krankhafte Geschwätzigkeit"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c7e610eb-3b0b-4d02-b5e5-439784896021",
                                "sense_id": "18258bee-4956-3ddd-aaf5-cbc61bb227e8",
                                "text": "„Logorrhö ist das Gegenteil von Mutismus, es ist keine sinnvolle Kommunikation möglich.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "9ec2fa33-c042-49d2-981a-6511b8478325",
                        "source_entry_id": "84cb31ce-963f-3ea7-998b-9157212a5e62",
                        "source_sense_id": "18258bee-4956-3ddd-aaf5-cbc61bb227e8",
                        "source_sense_description": "Psychologie: krankhafte Geschwätzigkeit",
                        "target_lang_word": "logorrhoea",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "logorrhoea",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5903f970-109a-30b0-bce1-b346bea4e92a",
                                        "entry_id": "c1c8df4d-42cd-3e30-a01e-88f56971e63b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative spelling of logorrhea."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK",
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9ba0a27e-ae8a-4947-862b-17df7394faed",
                                                "sense_id": "5903f970-109a-30b0-bce1-b346bea4e92a",
                                                "text": "The baritone is angry, but still controlled: he does not indulge in compulsive over-rapid spurts of logorrhoeas but keeps to a 'chopped, short, hard, very pointed' staccato-like delivery, excited, but well articulated through interruptions of differing lengths."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e956352d-06f9-4271-8af5-cead967542c9",
                                                "sense_id": "5903f970-109a-30b0-bce1-b346bea4e92a",
                                                "text": "The quantity of speech may be increased in mania and anxiety but reduced in dementia, schizophrenia and depression. [...] In logorrhoea, also called volubility, the speech is fluent and rambling, with the use of many words."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f5ae053e-2f63-4dc4-9afc-569ccaa39fca",
                                                "sense_id": "5903f970-109a-30b0-bce1-b346bea4e92a",
                                                "text": "His purchase of a Dictaphone no doubt encouraged his natural loquacity, his ingrained prolixity (which he himself logorrhoea)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e0132a38-479e-4c83-b586-418623de55b7",
                                                "sense_id": "5903f970-109a-30b0-bce1-b346bea4e92a",
                                                "text": "In many cases Philip [II of Spain] lapsed into a logorrhoea that not only revealed the thought processes that underlay his decisions but also shared details on his personal life – when and where he ate and slept; what he had just read; which trees and flowers he wanted to plant in his gardens (and where); how problems with his eyes, his legs or his wrist, or a cold or a headache, had made him fall behind with his paperwork."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "2454c7d3-6595-4a0e-85f8-8b2746568bb4",
                        "source_entry_id": "84cb31ce-963f-3ea7-998b-9157212a5e62",
                        "source_sense_id": "18258bee-4956-3ddd-aaf5-cbc61bb227e8",
                        "source_sense_description": "Psychologie: krankhafte Geschwätzigkeit",
                        "target_lang_word": "logorrhea",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "logorrhea",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2c8dd18a-07c2-3b2c-9cf9-ff5d670e658d",
                                        "entry_id": "32c93061-9c33-392a-850a-a23cabdb2930",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Excessive talkativeness."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US",
                                            "countable",
                                            "humorous",
                                            "often",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "631f6431-069d-4429-857b-dc72f264ba07",
                                                "sense_id": "2c8dd18a-07c2-3b2c-9cf9-ff5d670e658d",
                                                "text": "These \"Symbolists\" are characterised by unbounded vanity and self-sufficiency; they are highly emotional; their thinking is hazy and disconnected. They suffer from \"Logorrhea\" or \"sickly talkativeness,\" and are unable to perform any work which requires concentration and persistency."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e6d3779-4106-4125-89ba-7d080b8f6d59",
                                                "sense_id": "2c8dd18a-07c2-3b2c-9cf9-ff5d670e658d",
                                                "text": "[Jacques] Rivette, bluntly, suffers from a good case of logorrhea. Even if he had none of these rationales, he would still make long films. In interviews he speaks in endless, ebullient sentences that surround their subjects like spider's webs and sometimes suffocate them."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "771adb50-c9ae-43c6-a6c6-e4cca2aa7471",
                                                "sense_id": "2c8dd18a-07c2-3b2c-9cf9-ff5d670e658d",
                                                "text": "The baritone is angry, but still controlled: he does not indulge in compulsive over-rapid spurts of logorrhoeas but keeps to a 'chopped, short, hard, very pointed' staccato-like delivery, excited, but well articulated through interruptions of differing lengths."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fd98452d-d08f-415e-a895-9f7bf5a637a2",
                                                "sense_id": "2c8dd18a-07c2-3b2c-9cf9-ff5d670e658d",
                                                "text": "His purchase of a Dictaphone no doubt encouraged his natural loquacity, his ingrained prolixity (which he himself logorrhoea)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "68ab2712-bc22-3b4c-b3de-1e3718e0d53c",
                                        "entry_id": "32c93061-9c33-392a-850a-a23cabdb2930",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Excessive use of words in writing; prolixity."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US",
                                            "countable",
                                            "humorous",
                                            "often",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e50e77ad-cd29-44f2-8393-a064575a3b1a",
                                                "sense_id": "68ab2712-bc22-3b4c-b3de-1e3718e0d53c",
                                                "text": "The writer should endeavor to have his observations first of all, exact, then apposite, and finally as brief as the nature of the case will admit. [...] Logorrhea and irrelevancy are the bane of a society."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3520ce07-979c-4a64-b961-df5a8f8fc046",
                                                "sense_id": "68ab2712-bc22-3b4c-b3de-1e3718e0d53c",
                                                "text": "So when I settled down that day I wrote feverishly and I wrote joyously. I wrote until my fingers were cramped and my head was empty. I surrendered to a blithe logorrhea that left me contentedly limp and lax and in need of an hour or two of open air."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "79cfe8a4-6fa4-4dc0-ace3-f46b02f14c8b",
                                                "sense_id": "68ab2712-bc22-3b4c-b3de-1e3718e0d53c",
                                                "text": "The early period of glasnost' encouraged a variety of graphomania and logorrhea—from numerous letters to the newspapers to memoirs, \"true stories,\" opinions, and revelations of wide political range."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3012cd64-68d5-4d00-8109-fd3e8ec08207",
                                                "sense_id": "68ab2712-bc22-3b4c-b3de-1e3718e0d53c",
                                                "text": "What must be said may sometimes be difficult to say and may require lots and lots of language—hence [Jacques] Derrida's endless logorrheas—but Derrida never raises his hands to Heaven in despair because his reading experience would exceed what he wishes to say."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a5aaefc4-79c8-4db2-a863-d8ee31c9949a",
                                                "sense_id": "68ab2712-bc22-3b4c-b3de-1e3718e0d53c",
                                                "text": "In many cases Philip [II of Spain] lapsed into a logorrhoea that not only revealed the thought processes that underlay his decisions but also shared details on his personal life – when and where he ate and slept; what he had just read; which trees and flowers he wanted to plant in his gardens (and where); how problems with his eyes, his legs or his wrist, or a cold or a headache, had made him fall behind with his paperwork."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ca278019-4c65-40ca-9552-fc2665f14168",
                                                "sense_id": "68ab2712-bc22-3b4c-b3de-1e3718e0d53c",
                                                "text": "Thus, from the 70s on, all the wondrous legal forms of 'self-management negotiations' (dogovaranja) proved inefficient within toothless 'indicative planning' and a profit-bent capitalist market. It spawned unbelievable logorrheas, for example in the norms occasioned by the 1972–80 laws about the new 'delegate system' of elections [...]."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b417e81b-64c8-380e-b48f-e5c41b10f89c",
                                        "entry_id": "32c93061-9c33-392a-850a-a23cabdb2930",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Excessive and often uncontrollable speaking due to a mental disorder."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US",
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e823fc18-bf89-4927-8990-63aa37d2028a",
                                                "sense_id": "b417e81b-64c8-380e-b48f-e5c41b10f89c",
                                                "text": "But, then, these persons have not only a copia verborum as to knowledge, but a volubility sometimes amounting to a logorrhœa in expressing what they know—although that may not be much."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "018d0849-8f01-46e5-b10e-5ab81a88b6b4",
                                                "sense_id": "b417e81b-64c8-380e-b48f-e5c41b10f89c",
                                                "text": "When the patient was admitted to this hospital five years ago, the symptoms of excitement in the wide sense, violence, aggressiveness, destructiveness, logorrhœa, were in the foreground as they had been during the previous attacks."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5e53450a-5d77-4120-a3b1-bd6141368e39",
                                                "sense_id": "b417e81b-64c8-380e-b48f-e5c41b10f89c",
                                                "text": "In agreement with the general consensus of writers on the subject, they affirmed that logorrhea (a loss of control over the flow of speech and subsequent flood of verbiage often seen in adult Wernicke's aphasics) is not characteristic of aphasic children."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "00700929-4239-4701-adce-c4dba777bc30",
                                                "sense_id": "b417e81b-64c8-380e-b48f-e5c41b10f89c",
                                                "text": "The quantity of speech may be increased in mania and anxiety but reduced in dementia, schizophrenia and depression. [...] In logorrhoea, also called volubility, the speech is fluent and rambling, with the use of many words."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f931bee3-3184-4ca9-a721-2e0d775671fa",
                        "source_entry_id": "84cb31ce-963f-3ea7-998b-9157212a5e62",
                        "source_sense_id": "18258bee-4956-3ddd-aaf5-cbc61bb227e8",
                        "source_sense_description": "Psychologie: krankhafte Geschwätzigkeit",
                        "target_lang_word": "logorrhée",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "logorrhée",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9981c6c6-4af2-3a5e-861d-c256128d1d3c",
                                        "entry_id": "458b4f9c-810f-34f1-8be1-fbe04529210b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Trouble du langage caractérisé par un besoin irrésistible et morbide de parler."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c410902d-12a9-44a3-91a6-85c04d5d4798",
                                                "sense_id": "9981c6c6-4af2-3a5e-861d-c256128d1d3c",
                                                "text": "En étudiant la fonction du langage nous avons vu chez les psychasténiques des crises de bavardages et des tics de parole de même que des arrêts de la parole déterminés par des peurs ou par la timidité. Ces phénomènes n’étaient pas sans analogie avec les crises de logorrhée et le mutisme de l’autre névrose."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6cecf144-6f6e-3818-961c-f04d231db89f",
                                        "entry_id": "458b4f9c-810f-34f1-8be1-fbe04529210b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Long discours creux. Flux de paroles inutiles et incohérentes. Blabla prétentieux pour présenter des banalités ou une analyse plus ou moins cohérente et fondée."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "literary"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "df390bfa-dd6e-4cb3-a430-1a66177ad752",
                                                "sense_id": "6cecf144-6f6e-3818-961c-f04d231db89f",
                                                "text": "Il y a des gens qui vous envahissent avec leur logorrhée : on a la pénible impression d’être prisonnière de leurs mots."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "aa8beebf-810a-43f1-9605-48f52d549019",
                                                "sense_id": "6cecf144-6f6e-3818-961c-f04d231db89f",
                                                "text": "C'était la meilleure solution : fermer sa gueule et tourner les talons en direction des vestiaires sans même attendre qu'il ait fini de se vider de toute cette logorrhée mauvaise qui lui sortait de la bouche et qui venait d'on ne savait où."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7360965f-94eb-4870-be58-699981d8ca8a",
                                                "sense_id": "6cecf144-6f6e-3818-961c-f04d231db89f",
                                                "text": "On peut parler de la montagne qui accouche d’une souris ou d’une logorrhée à faire ronfler une carpe."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
